  "meta": {
    "title": "wcwᴿ — palindromes with a centre marker",
    "blurb": "A word, the marker c, then the word backwards. Because c announces the middle, no guessing is needed and a deterministic PDA suffices: push until c, then pop and compare. Remove the marker and determinism is lost — see “Palindromes: guess the middle”.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "c",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aca",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abcba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbcbba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abcab",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "copied, not mirrored"
      },
      {
        "w": "acca",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "two markers"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "palindromes",
        "deterministic-cfl",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "chapter": "Hopcroft, Motwani & Ullman, §6.4",
      "readme": "The deterministic context-free languages are the ones a PDA can parse without ever guessing, and **wcwᴿ** is the textbook member. Everything before the c is pushed; after it, each symbol must match the top of the stack.\n\nIts unmarked cousin **wwᴿ** (even palindromes) is context-free but *not* deterministic: a machine reading left to right cannot know where the middle is until it is too late. That gap — a language a nondeterministic PDA recognises and no deterministic one does — is one of the places where the pushdown world differs from the finite one, where determinism costs nothing."
    }
  }
}
